## Item SA-F3 (S1) — FIXED

Root cause (code-cited): the run loop's marquee "steady ticker" pin
branch compared `marquee_next_step(now) <= now` — but next_step is
STRICTLY FUTURE by construction (k = q+1 ⇒ start+k·80 > now), so the
pin NEVER fired. The clock advanced only on the plain 100ms branch;
wakes landed on every 80ms boundary, so the 100ms threshold tripped
every OTHER boundary → the clock jumped +160ms → offset +2 cells per
render → the measured ~166ms/step.

- marquee.rs: `passed_step_ms` — the most recent boundary ≤ now while
  the scroll is live (liveness mirrors next_step_ms; None past the
  one-pass clamp). Unit test pins the invariant pair (next is strictly
  future; pin+interval = next) and the clamp symmetry.
- session_tabs_solid.rs: `marquee_passed_step` accessor.
- app.rs tick section: while a scroll is live, wake → pin
  `advance_clock(passed_boundary)` when newer than the clock (compared
  against the CLOCK, never `now`); the plain 100ms tick stays suspended
  while scrolling (the 80ms pin cadence subsumes it — the clock never
  samples past a boundary the marquee should land on).
- vt_temporal.rs marquee test upgraded to the SA-F3 vt-clock proof:
  polls the LIVE grid every 12ms (the snapshot instrument's 80ms
  quiet window CANNOT resolve an 80ms cadence — consecutive frames
  merge into one burst; direct grid polling measures change times),
  takes the longest ≤300ms-separated steady run, asserts median ∈
  [70,105], max ≤ 140 (no ~166 quantization leak), min ≥ 55 (no
  double-steps), ≥10 shifts. MEASURED: 27 shifts, median 77ms, min
  76, max 90 (3/3 stable + full vt_temporal suite 5/5 PASS — the
  100ms-cadence surfaces unchanged).

## Item SA-F13 (S1) — FIXED

Root cause (code-cited): the input reader thread
(app.rs input-thread spawn) looped `crossterm::event::read()` forever;
it exits only when a key arrives AFTER its receiver dropped. A second
`run_with_size` in the process spawns its own reader, but the zombie
from boot 1 sits INSIDE crossterm's global reader lock
(INTERNAL_EVENT_READER, crossterm-0.28.1 event.rs:147) and consumes
the first key(s) — leg-2's ctrl+c is eaten, the loop never breaks,
the future never returns (the scan's dead-chord + teardown-hang).

- app.rs: the reader now `event::poll(50ms)`s against an
  `Arc<AtomicBool>` stop flag; `StopInputReader` (Drop guard held by
  the run future) fires it on the normal teardown AND on outer
  cancellation (a test timeout dropping the future); the boot's slot
  swap (`INPUT_READER_STOP`) stops any stale reader from an abandoned
  boot before spawning the new one.
- vt_double_boot.rs: the SA-F13 acceptance — TWO full boots in one
  process on ONE shared PTY (the scan repro's shape; NOTE: crossterm's
  global event source registers fd 0's open-file-description once per
  process, so a per-leg re-attach would test a harness artifact, not
  the app — the test reuses one LiveHarness): each leg proves the
  input path live (typed echo), then the FIRST exit chord must tear
  down and the run-loop future must return.
  * WITH the fix: PASS (2.7s, both legs return, chord tears down).
  * WITHOUT (git stash of app.rs): leg-1 returns, leg-2 NEVER exits
    ("the TUI did not exit in time") — the failure reproduced in both
    directions.
- Full oc-tui + oc-plugin-host: 899/899 PASS; clippy lib 0 warnings.

## S2 set — dispositioned (commit e891102)

### SA-F7 (`+` click creates no tab) — NOT-A-BUG, parity verified
Ref pairing verified from the ref source: the `+` release runs
`tabs.add()` which is `route.navigate({type:"home", …})`
(context/session-tabs.tsx:406-420) — the ref does NOT create a
session/tab on the click (the home composer's next submit does). Ours
already navigates Home (app.rs StripHit::Add); the existing test
`mouse_home_strip_click_selects_the_restored_tab` pins it — its
comment now carries the SA-F7 ref citation. No behavior change.

### SA-F14 (first chord ignored with a config FILE) — FIXED via SA-F13
Dedicated repro added (vt_config_chord.rs): a FIRST boot with cli.json
present (single chord must tear down) AND the canary's exact shape —
leg A (no config) → plant cli.json → leg B (config FILE), one shared
PTY (crossterm's global event source registers fd 0's
open-file-description ONCE per process — a per-leg re-attach tests a
harness artifact, not the app; learned from the first version of the
double-boot test). 3/3 stable PASS both legs. Combined with SA-F13's
falsifiability run (pre-fix: the SECOND boot's chord dead), the
disposition: the registered class was the input-reader zombie eating
the first chord on the canary's leg-B (a SECOND in-process boot — the
config FILE was incidental; the env-overlay probes were single boots
and always passed). vt_config_chord.rs stands guard on both shapes.

### SA-F16 (plugin-host uncaughtException on EACCES dirs) — FIXED
The EACCES throws escape from GUEST timer callbacks (the plugin's
periodic multiplexer/config probing walking /lost+found,
/tmp/tmux-*, …) through bun's task queue to the process handler.
Hardening (oc-plugin-host, host.js): every scheduled guest callback
(setInterval/setTimeout) runs through a shield — sync throws and
async rejections degrade to a `plugin.fault` notification (+ a warn
log), never an uncaught process-level throw; timer handles and return
values preserved. supervisor.rs records the faults (bounded, newest
first) as health results; PluginHost::plugin_faults() exposes them.
Test timer_faults.rs (fixture timercrasher.ts: a 60ms sync-EACCES
probe + a 90ms async rejection): faults recorded for BOTH arms, the
faulting callback stops at the throw, dispatch stays live. PASS;
WITHOUT the shields (git stash) the test FAILS (faults list empty).

